<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Cahokia</h1><p class="st-meta">1050 CE – 1350 CE (approx.) · capital Cahokia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest city in North America north of Mexico before the 18th century, whose Monks Mound remains the largest prehistoric earthwork in the Americas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1050 a cluster of villages on the American Bottom, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> floodplain opposite modern St Louis, was rapidly rebuilt to a grand plan. Archaeologists call this sudden change the Big Bang: a central plaza was levelled, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monks-mound">Monks Mound</a> was raised, and thousands of people gathered in what was likely the first Mississippian capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>At its peak around 1100 Cahokia may have housed 10,000 to 20,000 people, fed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> farming. Rulers lived atop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monks-mound">Monks Mound</a>, some 30 metres high, and circles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> posts, the so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cahokia-woodhenge">Woodhenge</a>, marked solar events. Mound 72 held a high-status burial on a bed of 20,000 shell <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, with many sacrificed people. Cahokian pottery, stone figurines and the chunkey game spread Mississippian ideas across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midwestern-united-states">Midwest</a> and South.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Cahokia declined after about 1200, when a palisade was built around its core, and was largely abandoned by 1350; flooding, drought, exhausted resources and political strife have all been proposed. Its name comes from a later subgroup of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/illinois-confederation">Illinois Confederation</a>. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1982.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cahokia.jpg" alt="Cahokia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Skubasteve834 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monks_Mound_-_Summer_2024.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1050 CE – 1350 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cahokia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mississippian religion</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cahokia chiefdom, Cahokia Mounds</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1200&amp;focus=cahokia" aria-label="Cahokia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2444 790 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cahokia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2764 1046h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2764 1046h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1200&amp;focus=cahokia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

